{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 199,
    "dDurationMs": 4901,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To understand where we came from—how earth,\nthe solar system, the galaxy became what they"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5100,
    "dDurationMs": 3800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are today—we need to understand how it all began."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 8900,
    "dDurationMs": 4760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For example, how did the first galaxies pull\nthemselves together from the dark universe-filling"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 13660,
    "dDurationMs": 2515,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ocean of gas that followed the Big Bang?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 16175,
    "dDurationMs": 4024,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "With the James Webb Space Telescope we’re\nstarting to be able to find those first galaxies."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 20199,
    "dDurationMs": 6730,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s hard work because at those crazy distances\nall we see is tiny, faint and fuzzy blobs."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 26929,
    "dDurationMs": 4661,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If only we could see the individual stars\nin those galaxies we could learn so much more."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 31590,
    "dDurationMs": 3990,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well, now using this one weird trick we can\ndo exactly that."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 35580,
    "dDurationMs": 5700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Or at least we have done so for one lonely star at the\nend of the universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 41280,
    "dDurationMs": 2042,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But it won’t be lonely for long."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 48829,
    "dDurationMs": 5070,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A top contender for most distant star we can\nsee with the naked eye is a huge variable"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 53899,
    "dDurationMs": 2511,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "star in the constellation Cassiopeiae."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 56410,
    "dDurationMs": 6202,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s 16,000 light years away, so just a\nfraction of the distance across the Milky Way."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 62612,
    "dDurationMs": 5388,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Stars more distant blend together into the\nunresolved Milky blur that gives our more"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 68000,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "galaxy its name."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 69000,
    "dDurationMs": 2790,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We can pick out those more distant stars with\ntelescopes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 71790,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But it was only a bit over a century ago that\nour telescopes got good enough to pick out"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 76950,
    "dDurationMs": 5279,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the brightest individual stars from our nearest\nsizable neighboring galaxy, Andromeda."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 82229,
    "dDurationMs": 4621,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "As our telescopes improved we were able to\nresolve those individual pinpricks of light"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 86850,
    "dDurationMs": 1550,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to greater and greater distances."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 88400,
    "dDurationMs": 2390,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But it feels like there’s a fundamental\nlimit."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 90790,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Our telescopes can only get so big, they can\nonly stare for so long."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 95430,
    "dDurationMs": 5070,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The most distant galaxies seem destined to\nremain faint fuzzy blobs for a very long time"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 100500,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to come."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 101500,
    "dDurationMs": 3860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Unless, but some happy chance, an individual\nstar at the end of the universe happens to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 105360,
    "dDurationMs": 5710,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "be directly behind an entire cluster of galaxies,\nresulting in an enormous amplification of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 111070,
    "dDurationMs": 1880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "its light by gravitational lensing."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 112950,
    "dDurationMs": 3419,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And that happy chance has happened."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 116369,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Meet Earendel - the farthest star ever seen\n- shining out from 28 billion light years"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 122689,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "distant."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 123689,
    "dDurationMs": 6061,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now that’s double the distance of the previous\nrecord holder, and it may even be our very"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 129750,
    "dDurationMs": 5420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "first direct glimpse of the first generation\nof stars to form in our universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 135170,
    "dDurationMs": 5510,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Earendel was first spotted by the Hubble Space\nTelescope in March 2022."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 140680,
    "dDurationMs": 7380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It was a lucky find, showing up as an inexplicably\nbright spot in the Reionization Lensing Cluster"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 148060,
    "dDurationMs": 1940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Survey, or “RELICS.”"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 150000,
    "dDurationMs": 6280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The RELICS was all about finding extremely\ndistant galaxies—galaxies whose light has"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 156280,
    "dDurationMs": 3420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "been traveling to us for most of the age of\nthe universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 159700,
    "dDurationMs": 5970,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "By peering back in time this way, we can learn\nhow galaxies first began to grow out of the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 165670,
    "dDurationMs": 3968,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ocean of dark gas that filled the universe\nafter the Big Bang."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 169638,
    "dDurationMs": 5562,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Studying the early galaxies is one of the main goals of the James Webb Space Telescope, but with some extra"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 175200,
    "dDurationMs": 5220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "cleverness the older Hubble can do some of\nthis work also."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 180420,
    "dDurationMs": 6620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It needed a bit of help to see that far, in\nthis case in the form of an additional lens—a"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 187040,
    "dDurationMs": 1770,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "gravitational lens."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 188810,
    "dDurationMs": 5770,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "RELICS looked towards and through clusters\nof galaxies, searching for vastly more distant"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 194580,
    "dDurationMs": 6577,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "galaxies behind clusters whose light is warped\nand magnified by the cluster’s gravitational field."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 201157,
    "dDurationMs": 4783,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "With this trick, the RELICS team\nmanaged to discover hundreds of galaxies from"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 205940,
    "dDurationMs": 3220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the first billion years of the universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 209160,
    "dDurationMs": 4150,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And in one of those galaxies a single star\nshone out."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 213310,
    "dDurationMs": 5140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It seemed impossibly bright, even under the\nmagnification that enhanced the rest of that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 218450,
    "dDurationMs": 1090,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "galaxy’s light."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 219540,
    "dDurationMs": 2460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They named the star ‘Earendel’."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 222000,
    "dDurationMs": 4330,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s a good name, and one with a deep\nand nerdy pedigree."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 226330,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s the old English for “morning star,”\nlikely another old name for Venus or maybe"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 231330,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "even sunrises."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 232330,
    "dDurationMs": 6210,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It probably comes from Old Norse, Aurvandill,\na mythological character who’s toe froze"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 238540,
    "dDurationMs": 6550,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "off and Thor tossed into the sky to make a\n“star” - again, probably Venus."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 245090,
    "dDurationMs": 1979,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That was all new knowledge to me."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 247069,
    "dDurationMs": 9604,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I knew the name in the alternate spelling Earendil. A name borrowed by Tolkien for a character in his Silmarillion."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 256673,
    "dDurationMs": 2917,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You know, the half-elven Earendil the Mariner,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 259590,
    "dDurationMs": 4130,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Father of Elrond and Elros, who wears one\nof the silmarils on his brow as he sails the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 263720,
    "dDurationMs": 4210,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sky, and that’s Tolkien’s Venus creation\nstory."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 267930,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A little more romantic perhaps than a frost-bitten\nbig toe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 271850,
    "dDurationMs": 5370,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But either way, Earendel-dil is a good name for\na very special star."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 277220,
    "dDurationMs": 2030,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And a distant one."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 279250,
    "dDurationMs": 2280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Earendel is 28 billion light years away."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 281530,
    "dDurationMs": 4850,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now astronomers usually don’t talk about\nlight years when the numbers for distance get so large."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 286380,
    "dDurationMs": 4710,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For such distant objects we just refer to\nthe redshift."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 291090,
    "dDurationMs": 4010,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Because the universe is expanding, a photon\ntraveling between galaxies gets stretched—its"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 295100,
    "dDurationMs": 1330,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "wavelength increases."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 296430,
    "dDurationMs": 3510,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The more time it spends traveling, the more\nit’s stretched."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 299940,
    "dDurationMs": 4270,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So if we measure this redshift of light we\nknow how long ago it must have left its starting"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 304210,
    "dDurationMs": 1010,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "point."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 305220,
    "dDurationMs": 2330,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That also tells us the distance to that starting\npoint."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 307550,
    "dDurationMs": 5450,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For comparison, light that’s been traveling\nto us for 10% of the age of the universe is"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 313000,
    "dDurationMs": 4280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "stretched by about the same factor, giving\nit a redshift of 0.1."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 317280,
    "dDurationMs": 7910,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Earendel’s redshift is 6.2—it’s light\nis stretched by a factor of more than 7."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 325190,
    "dDurationMs": 4530,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That gigantic redshift means its light must\nhave been traveling for nearly 13 billion"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 329720,
    "dDurationMs": 2340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "years—most of the age of the universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 332060,
    "dDurationMs": 5560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The star is now 28 billion light years away\nrather than 13 billion because while its light"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 337620,
    "dDurationMs": 5070,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "was traveling to us, it was racing away from\nus with the expanding universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 342690,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "At this crazy redshift, all of Earendel’s\nvisible light and a lot of its ultraviolet"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 347490,
    "dDurationMs": 2870,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "reaches us as infrared light."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 350360,
    "dDurationMs": 5279,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Hubble is optimized for visible light, so\nit can only pick up a small fraction of Earendel’s"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 355639,
    "dDurationMs": 6280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "light—just its shortest wavelength ultraviolet\nthat ends up still being in the range of Hubble’s"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 361919,
    "dDurationMs": 1051,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sensitivity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 362970,
    "dDurationMs": 3890,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now, it sure would be nice if we had a space telescope even bigger than Hubble that was sensitive"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 366860,
    "dDurationMs": 1610,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to infrared light."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 368470,
    "dDurationMs": 1120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well what do you know?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 369590,
    "dDurationMs": 1190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We do."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 370780,
    "dDurationMs": 4169,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The James Webb Space Telescope has a collecting\narea more than five times larger than hubble,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 374949,
    "dDurationMs": 3695,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and was designed especially for work deep\ninto the infrared spectrum"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 378644,
    "dDurationMs": 2716,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "allowing it to see galaxies in the early universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 381360,
    "dDurationMs": 5029,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Seeing one of the individual stars inside one of these\ngalaxies is a huge bonus."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 386389,
    "dDurationMs": 4731,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Here’s the Hubble image of Earendel and\nhere’s the Webb image."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 391120,
    "dDurationMs": 6931,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This faint streak on either side is the star’s\nhome galaxy, fittingly named the ‘Sunrise Arc.'"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 398051,
    "dDurationMs": 2359,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The galaxy doesn’t really look like this."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 400410,
    "dDurationMs": 5650,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It would be a much more compact bundle of\nbillions of stars, but its light has been smeared"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 406060,
    "dDurationMs": 4840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "across the sky by the vast gravitational field\nof this cluster of galaxies that you can see"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 410900,
    "dDurationMs": 1890,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in the foreground."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 412790,
    "dDurationMs": 5800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Smeared and amplified—and the amplification\nis why the RELICS program looked at this thing"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 418590,
    "dDurationMs": 1530,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in the first place."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 420120,
    "dDurationMs": 2280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Gravitational lensing is really an incredible\ntool."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 422400,
    "dDurationMs": 3030,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It lets us see much further than we otherwise\ncould."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 425430,
    "dDurationMs": 5970,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s do a quick review of how this works.In\ngravitational lensing, rays of light from"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 431400,
    "dDurationMs": 5229,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a distant object that would normally not reach\nour telescope are deflected toawards us by"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 436629,
    "dDurationMs": 2801,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "an intervening gravitational field."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 439430,
    "dDurationMs": 4870,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In the case of so-called weak lensing, this\njust leads to a distortion of the image, like"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 444300,
    "dDurationMs": 2250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "looking through rippled water."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 446550,
    "dDurationMs": 4430,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But if the alignment of the lens and the source\nis just right then many light rays that would"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 450980,
    "dDurationMs": 4170,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "otherwise have traveled in very different\ndirections are deflected towards us."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 455150,
    "dDurationMs": 2150,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is called strong lensing."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 457300,
    "dDurationMs": 4770,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In the case of a lens with perfect circular\nsymmetry and a perfect alignment of us and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 462070,
    "dDurationMs": 4220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "lens and source, rays traveling in all different\ndirections reach us."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 466290,
    "dDurationMs": 5439,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now the warped image looks like a perfect\nring around the lens—what we call an Einstein"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 471729,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ring."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 472729,
    "dDurationMs": 3081,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We do see Einstein rings, but they’re rare."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 475810,
    "dDurationMs": 4350,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Normally the alignment isn’t quite perfect\nand the lens isn’t symmetric enough."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 480160,
    "dDurationMs": 2550,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Break either of those and the ring fragments."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 482710,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For example, into multiple images for very\ncompact sources, or multiple arcs for extended"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 487910,
    "dDurationMs": 1810,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sources."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 489720,
    "dDurationMs": 2240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Individual lensed images are also magnified."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 491960,
    "dDurationMs": 5660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Just like a glass lens can bend diverging\nrays of light back towards each other to a"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 497620,
    "dDurationMs": 4930,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "focus, thereby increasing brightness, a gravitational\nlens can do the same."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 502550,
    "dDurationMs": 5149,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For a glass lens the magnification is the\ngreatest if the light source is at the focal"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 507699,
    "dDurationMs": 5661,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "point relative to your eye—then all diverging\nrays are sent straight to your pupil."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 513360,
    "dDurationMs": 3300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Gravitational lenses are messier, and instead\nof a focal point you'll"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 516660,
    "dDurationMs": 2250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "have a focal curve called a caustic."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 518910,
    "dDurationMs": 5549,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They’ll be this star-like structure for a\ntypical lensing configuration with a relatively"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 524459,
    "dDurationMs": 1541,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "simple gravitational field."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 526000,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You see similar shapes when you shine light\nthrough the stem of a wine class, or through"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 531080,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "rippled water."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 532080,
    "dDurationMs": 4324,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The caustic curve represents boundaries in the plane of the source."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 536404,
    "dDurationMs": 5449,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "When the source crosses those boundaries new images appear or vanish."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 541853,
    "dDurationMs": 6644,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "As the source approaches a caustic curve, multiple images can come together and overlap as they merge."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 548497,
    "dDurationMs": 4772,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Leading to a large increase in the amplification of it's brightness."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 553269,
    "dDurationMs": 2411,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And that’s exactly what happened with Earendel."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 555680,
    "dDurationMs": 3670,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In this case it’s very near the caustic\ncurve produced by"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 559350,
    "dDurationMs": 2739,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "one of the galaxies in its cluster."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 562089,
    "dDurationMs": 7511,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The rest of Earendel's home galaxy spans extends far beyondthis particular caustic, and is actually being"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 569600,
    "dDurationMs": 5500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "magnified and smeared by the gravitational\nfield of the entire cluster to a much lesser"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 575100,
    "dDurationMs": 1239,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "degree than Earendel is."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 576339,
    "dDurationMs": 6991,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But this star, with its happy position, experiences\nmagnification of at least a factor of 1,000,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 583330,
    "dDurationMs": 3670,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and could be up to 40,000."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 587000,
    "dDurationMs": 5060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "At the lower limit, we’re gaining the light\ncollecting power of a telescope 30 times larger"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 592060,
    "dDurationMs": 2339,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in diameter."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 594399,
    "dDurationMs": 6500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "On either side of Earendel, you can also see one of\nthe lensed image of a nearby cluster of stars"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 600899,
    "dDurationMs": 1281,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in the same galaxy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 602180,
    "dDurationMs": 4571,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That cluster is close to the caustic curve,\nbut not as close as Earendel."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 606751,
    "dDurationMs": 4479,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "As a result its multiple images are sufficiently\nseparated to be visible."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 611230,
    "dDurationMs": 5430,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "OK, so now we’ve found this distant\nstar, what can we learn about it?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 616660,
    "dDurationMs": 4810,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Like I said, Hubble was only able to capture\na fraction of the star’s light due to most"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 621470,
    "dDurationMs": 3369,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of that light being redshifted outside Hubble’s\nrange."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 624839,
    "dDurationMs": 4521,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But Webb is sensitive to much of Earendel’s\nnow-infrared light."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 629360,
    "dDurationMs": 3450,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Here’s what we know so far."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 632810,
    "dDurationMs": 4120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "“De-redshifting” the light we can compare\nthe spectrum—essentially the raw colour"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 636930,
    "dDurationMs": 4130,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the star—to known stellar types."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 641060,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Earendel appears to be a blue-ish white type\nB main sequence star, that makes it at least 50"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 647860,
    "dDurationMs": 5990,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "times more massive than the Sun with a surface\nof more than 20,000 Kelvin."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 653850,
    "dDurationMs": 4910,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "On top of that, there also seems to be an\nexcess of red light coming from Earendel,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 658760,
    "dDurationMs": 3080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which astronomers think could be due to a\ncompanion star."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 661840,
    "dDurationMs": 2280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This isn’t unusual."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 664120,
    "dDurationMs": 5500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "50 solar mass stars often have binary\npartners, but the mixing of the light from"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 669620,
    "dDurationMs": 5620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the two stars combined with the uncertain\nmagnification makes it difficult to say exactly"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 675240,
    "dDurationMs": 2899,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "how massive and luminous each star is."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 678139,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "One proposed explanation is that\nthe main blue star of this binary pair shines"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 683139,
    "dDurationMs": 6811,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "about 600,000 times brighter than the sun, while\nthe companion is a red giant star shining"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 689950,
    "dDurationMs": 2980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "about 200,000 times brighter than\nthe sun."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 692930,
    "dDurationMs": 2659,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Earendel is important for a bunch of reasons."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 695589,
    "dDurationMs": 5651,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For example, both it and its newly discovered\ncompanion are the first direct data points"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 701240,
    "dDurationMs": 2750,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we have for the earliest stars."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 703990,
    "dDurationMs": 4150,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We know a lot about star formation in the\nuniverse today because we can actually see"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 708140,
    "dDurationMs": 1150,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it happening."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 709290,
    "dDurationMs": 4900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We have countless snapshots of star formation\nin action in star-forming clouds, both in"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 714190,
    "dDurationMs": 3730,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the Milky Way and in relatively nearby galaxies."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 717920,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Meanwhile our surveys of individual stars across the\nMilky Way and beyond tell us the final results"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 723800,
    "dDurationMs": 2860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the star formation process."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 726660,
    "dDurationMs": 5300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We can check our calculations and computer\nsimulations against these observations to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 731960,
    "dDurationMs": 2590,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "figure out how modern star formation works."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 734550,
    "dDurationMs": 3979,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "While we can still do computer simulations\nof star formation in the early universe, there’s"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 738529,
    "dDurationMs": 3171,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "not much to check those simulations against."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 741700,
    "dDurationMs": 4520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But still, those simulations have revealed tantalizing\npossibilities."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 746220,
    "dDurationMs": 6780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For example, we know that the gas from which these\nstars formed back then must have been more"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 753000,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "“pure” — almost entirely hydrogen and\nhelium."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 756920,
    "dDurationMs": 5320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These days that gas is polluted by heavier\nelements produced in the deaths of multiple"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 762240,
    "dDurationMs": 1730,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "generations of stars."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 763970,
    "dDurationMs": 6760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Computer simulations show that this early,\npristine gas should've produce more massive stars"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 770730,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "than later, polluted gas—something we’ve\ndiscussed previously."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 774810,
    "dDurationMs": 5510,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In the Milky Way today around 3% of new stars have masses 10 times the sun’s"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 780320,
    "dDurationMs": 1560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mass or higher."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 781880,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The majority will be relatively low mass stars\nlike our sun or even smaller."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 787320,
    "dDurationMs": 5340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "On the other hand, simulations of early galaxies\ntell us as many as 20% of stars might have"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 792660,
    "dDurationMs": 1869,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "more than 10 solar masses."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 794529,
    "dDurationMs": 6341,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So really hefty stars like Earendel may have\nbeen more of a norm than an exception in the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 800870,
    "dDurationMs": 2260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "early universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 803130,
    "dDurationMs": 5660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Being around when the universe is 900 million\nyears old makes it possible, but unlikely,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 808790,
    "dDurationMs": 4140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that Earendel is one of the very first generation of\nstars."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 812930,
    "dDurationMs": 1290,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That would be truly incredible."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 814220,
    "dDurationMs": 3580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We’ve never seen one of the founding batch\nbefore."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 817800,
    "dDurationMs": 5780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But even being from an early generation of\nstars means we can learn important"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 823580,
    "dDurationMs": 1910,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "things about this star's parents."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 825490,
    "dDurationMs": 5649,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "All of this is really important for understanding\nhow our modern very sparkly universe of stars"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 831139,
    "dDurationMs": 3176,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and galaxies pulled itself together from the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 834315,
    "dDurationMs": 2413,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "gas that existed after the big bang."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 836728,
    "dDurationMs": 3142,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Earendel is not a totally solved star."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 839870,
    "dDurationMs": 4870,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We don’t know how much magnification it’s\nexperiencing, so can’t know its true brightness"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 844740,
    "dDurationMs": 3060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "until we model the lens a lot better."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 847800,
    "dDurationMs": 4529,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And its fainter red companion is even more\nmysterious."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 852329,
    "dDurationMs": 3510,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But this is a seriously exciting new line\nof investigation."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 855839,
    "dDurationMs": 4370,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "While we can’t say a ton yet from the current\nobservations, further observations of this"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 860209,
    "dDurationMs": 4391,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "star and the discovery of new objects like\nit could be revelatory."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 864600,
    "dDurationMs": 5330,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For example, if we can get better information\nabout the spectrum of the star we may be able"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 869930,
    "dDurationMs": 4659,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to tell whether it really does have the low\nabundance of heavy elements expected of an"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 874589,
    "dDurationMs": 1071,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "early star."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 875660,
    "dDurationMs": 4790,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Perhaps we even confirm that it’s mostly\nraw hydrogen and helium, increasing the chance"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 880450,
    "dDurationMs": 3319,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that it being a first generation star."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 883769,
    "dDurationMs": 5690,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If we can find more lensed stars like this\nthen we can start to build up population statistics."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 889459,
    "dDurationMs": 4930,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That can tell us if massive stars really were\nproduced in larger quantities back then."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 894389,
    "dDurationMs": 4357,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The specifics of the resulting population\ncould tell us a ton about"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 898746,
    "dDurationMs": 2765,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "star formation in the early universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 901511,
    "dDurationMs": 7078,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now upcoming giant surveys of the sky are definitely\ngoing to find, like, millions of lensed galaxies."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 908589,
    "dDurationMs": 5261,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Hopefully some of them will have a star conveniently\nlocated near a caustic curve, enabling us"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 913850,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to see that star."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 914850,
    "dDurationMs": 7130,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I do enjoy the severed-digit themed origin stories\nof the Norse, and especially like the elves-sailing-sky-ships"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 921980,
    "dDurationMs": 1840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "origin story of Tolkien."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 923820,
    "dDurationMs": 5980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But as our new and next generations of incredible\nobservatories peer deeper into the cosmic"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 929800,
    "dDurationMs": 4310,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "dawn, we no longer have to invent our own\norigin story."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 934110,
    "dDurationMs": 1419,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We can know it."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 935529,
    "dDurationMs": 5990,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Will the help of Earendel, the star at the\nbeginning of the universe and at the edge"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 941519,
    "dDurationMs": 1391,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of observable spacetime."
    } ]
  } ]
}
